    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe rule: reject `POST /api/owners` with **409 Conflict** when the owner's city already holds 50+ owners.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `CityCapacity.java` (small, cohesive helper, mirroring the existing `Namesakes` / `CustomerCodes` / `HouseholdDuplicates` pattern in that package):\n\n```java\nstatic final int LIMIT = 50;\nstatic boolean isAtCapacity(Owner candidate, Collection<Owner> existing) {\n    long inCity = existing.stream()\n        .filter(other -> candidate.getCity().equalsIgnoreCase(other.getCity()))\n        .count();\n    return inCity >= LIMIT;\n}\n```\n\n**One-clause edit** to `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` \u2014 folded the check into the existing conflict guard, reusing the `existingOwners` collection already fetched there:\n\n```java\nif (telephoneTaken || CityCapacity.isAtCapacity(owner, existingOwners)\n    || HouseholdDuplicates.isRejectedDuplicate(...)) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);\n}\n```\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n- The city-counting logic lives in a **brand-new tiny class** (WMC_other \u2248 0), not inside the large controller.\n- The only change to the expensive `addOwner` method is **two edited lines** adding a single boolean clause, so its CC barely moves and `changed_lines` stays minimal.\n- City comparison is case-insensitive, matching the existing `CustomerCodes` convention.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
